From 0400f9e51ad74d5191b02744eaf705c23efb03d4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Rasmus Widing <rasmus.widing@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 10 Aug 2026 23:33:28 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] fix(clipboard): honour the clipboard a write names
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

`writeClipboard` bound its `clipboard: ghostty_clipboard_e` argument to
`_`, so every write — whichever clipboard it named — landed on
`NSPasteboard.general`. A program emitting `OSC 52` at the selection
target therefore replaced the user's system clipboard, silently.

Apple platforms have exactly one pasteboard. `selection` and `primary`
are X11 buffers with nothing to map them onto, so a write aimed at one
is now refused rather than redirected — resolved once through a new
`TerminalClipboardDestination` instead of by a parameter nobody reads.

`unknown` is reachable rather than defensive: ghostty.h declares two
constants while ghostty's own `apprt.Clipboard` has three and passes
`@intFromEnum` across the boundary, so `OSC 52 ;p;` arrives as a raw
`2` matching neither — and reached the pasteboard before this.

Also stops claiming a selection clipboard in the runtime config, which
is what Ghostty.app answers and what makes `copy-on-select = true`
target the pasteboard this host can actually address. That flag alone
does not close the OSC 52 route: ghostty consults it only for the
copy-on-select target and the middle-click paste source, never in
`Surface.clipboardWrite` — hence the resolution in the callback.

The read direction deliberately gets no matching guard. ghostty calls
`startClipboardRequest(.standard, .{ .osc_52_read = clipboard })`, so
every read arrives as `.standard` whatever the program asked for —
verified by inverting the guard, which stopped `;c;?`, `;s;?` and
`;p;?` alike. The only caller that passes a real choice is middle-click
paste, and it consults `supportsClipboard(.selection)` first, so with
the flag above it resolves to `.standard` too. A guard there could only
ever break a paste the user asked for.

`confirm` is likewise still unread; its two honest answers are both a
policy for the embedding app rather than for this wrapper.
